{"kind":"assert","text":"For Hugh: make evidence reuse explicit in the first review packet. Two recent reviews found avoidable attribution errors even though useful underlying evidence already existed. I recommend a small reporting convention, with no new approval stage or default test rerun.\n\nAn implementation artifact should distinguish checks executed on this candidate from evidence carried from an earlier head. Each group links to its actual execution record, naming the source head, command, toolchain and outcome. For carried evidence, state why the intervening change leaves that check relevant. Copy these facts from the retained record rather than retyping a blanket “all checks passed at this head” claim. A mutant that failed to compile must remain distinct from a compiled behavioral mutation that a test caught.\n\nThe [two review findings and corrected example](evidence-attribution-cases.json) show the cost. CI review #21825 accepted the source correction but required another review round because an unchanged old-head transcript was described as new execution. The corrected #21827 artifact separated those records and the same source head later landed. Scale review #21945 found mixed toolchain attribution, a compile-failing mutant described as a behavioral rejection, and a gate log described at a later head. These findings do not invalidate every retained result; accurate attribution makes reuse defensible.\n\nFor expensive measurements, publish the intended target table and interpretation rules at an exact revision before starting, using the existing commitment and artifact mechanism. Keep the measured head separate from the later reporting head. A [retained rules file](performance/README.md@cbc3b81bd77424e6b141856264af7432c8a3c4f4:140) cannot alone prove it preceded a run. If that evidence is absent, say so and obtain an explicit requester disposition; do not invent pre-registration or repeat a large campaign just to hide the gap.\n\nTry this on the next three implementation packets. Count review returns caused by evidence attribution and avoidable reruns; compare those counts with the small preparation cost. The two cases establish a recurring problem, not a measured time saving. This complements #21241's complete-diff review packet recommendation and does not change review authority, exact-head approval or merge safeguards.","body":{"head":"cbc3b81bd77424e6b141856264af7432c8a3c4f4","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4bd740742bf0b3b3dfaed8338baf76d085dff33a, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:84ecd0f16c8efa6bb6abe3d106d348d2c4fd8549 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:00925ae6c09fb0b8e2a72e84c22ca3488bc9534d, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:015aba51ab6c2416861618c5ac3c9ae0e444f17f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:063ac469f4327eef88a6cef497d6b24b5d128af7, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:069ba281df08c36e7b19c66532ae24357887a9e5 and 97 more"}}